Scope of the Reference Implementation

This release includes:

• structural media definitions
• output visibility rules
• minimal executable reference (video generation script)
• output demonstration

STRUMER defines a deterministic structural model where video output emerges from structure:

resolve(structure) ->
RESOLVED if structure is complete AND consistent
ABSTAIN  if structure is incomplete
BLOCKED  if structure is inconsistent

What This Demonstrates

• video generation without editing dependency
• independence from timeline workflows
• independence from manual adjustment
• independence from tools as a requirement for output
• structural resolution governing output visibility
• safe handling of incomplete and conflicting structure
• deterministic and reproducible media output
• identical structure produces identical video output

Conformance

An implementation may be considered compatible if it preserves:

• structure-based output
• independence from editing as a requirement for output
• deterministic resolution
• structure-driven output visibility
• reproducibility under equivalent inputs
• explicit handling of incomplete and conflicting structure

Conformance is judged solely by observable behavior — not by internal implementation.

Conflict Handling

In this reference implementation:

• BLOCKED is explicitly defined and demonstrated
• ABSTAIN corresponds to incomplete structure (no output)
• inconsistent conditions do not produce visible output

Conforming behavior ensures:

• no forced output under incomplete structure
• no arbitrary output under conflicting structure

Dependency Independence Guarantee

Output is invariant under tool or environment state:

resolve(S, D1) = resolve(S, D2)

for all dependency states.

This ensures:

dependency_failure != output_failure

Output depends only on structure — not tool state.

Relationship to Shunyaya Framework

STRUMER is part of the broader Shunyaya framework.

It builds on:

• SLANG -> correctness without execution
• ORL -> correctness without ordering
• STIME -> correctness without time
• STINT -> correctness without connectivity
• STILE -> correctness without communication
• SVARE -> correctness without computation
• STRUMER -> output without editing

Each removes a dependency.
Output remains preserved by structure.

